course: everyday
lesson: 352
title: Taking too much
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: At Maisie's party in Bristol, Finn ate nearly all the sweets in the bowl while the others played pass the parcel. When the game ended, the bowl was empty and a little boy cried. Maisie's mum told Finn's mum at pick-up, and Finn went red all the way home. He said sorry to Maisie, and for the next party he brought a bag of sweets to share and handed them round first.
explain: Ella grabbed the biggest slice of birthday cake before anyone else could. Her little sister got the thin end and cried, and Mum made them swap. Ella sulked — «it's not fair, I saw it first» — but at bedtime she said sorry, and the next time there was cake she let her sister choose first.
explain: Greed is wanting more than your share; envy is being sore that someone else has something good. Both make you do things that leave others with less, and both spoil the very treat you grabbed.
explain: Sometimes greed goes unnoticed — nobody counts the pencils — but the person left with the broken ones still notices. Being caught or not caught does not change who went without.
fact: When one person takes too much of something shared, others are left with little or nothing.
fact: Envy can make you say mean things you do not really believe, like calling something ugly because you want it. Kai called his friend's new trainers ugly because he secretly wanted them, and his friend, sad, stopped wearing them.
fact: Accusing someone of cheating because you are jealous of their win is unfair and can make them cry.
fact: Pushing in a queue can make someone fall and get hurt; the fair fix is to say sorry and go to the back.
fact: Shared class things, like a pencil box, belong to everyone in the class.
fact: Hiding treats from a brother or sister usually spoils the treat, because guilt comes with it.
fact: Not sharing a new toy because it might break can send a friend home early, not wanting to play again; sharing it and showing how to use it gently is wiser.
fact: Being happy for someone else's success is a skill that gets easier with practice.
fact: A good apology names what you did and then makes it fair, for example by sharing or giving back.
fact: Letting someone else choose first is a simple way to put right a grab.
exercise: Why did Finn eat nearly all the sweets in the bowl? | He wanted them and did not think of the others | Maisie's mum told him to finish them off before tea | He was sharing them with friends | He was trying to help clear up
exercise: What happened because Finn took too much? | The other children had no sweets and one cried | Everyone had more sweets than before, because he shared | Nobody noticed the empty bowl | Maisie's mum bought him more
exercise: What best put Finn's greed right? | Saying sorry and sharing sweets at the next party | Hiding the wrappers under the sofa so nobody could tell | Saying the bowl was too small | Never going to a party again
exercise: Kai said his friend's new trainers were ugly because he secretly wanted them. Which feeling made him say it? | Envy | Kindness | Boredom | Fear of the dark
exercise: What happened after Kai's mean comment? | His friend stopped wearing the trainers and was sad | His friend laughed and agreed, then gave him the trainers | His friend gave him the trainers | Nothing changed at all
exercise: Ella grabbed the biggest slice. Why did Mum make the sisters swap? | To make it fair after the grab | Because Mum wanted the big slice | Because big slices are unhealthy | Because the little one was older
exercise: Ella said «it's not fair, I saw it first». Is seeing it first a fair reason to take the biggest piece? | No, seeing it first does not make it yours | Yes, the first to see it wins, that's the rule | Yes, if you are older | Only on your own birthday
exercise: Oscar would not let his friend play with his new toy because he was scared it would break. What happened? | His friend went home early and did not want to play again | His friend thanked him for being so careful with the special toy | His friend bought his own toy | The toy broke anyway
exercise: What was a wiser choice for Oscar? | Share the toy and show how to use it gently | Hide the toy whenever friends come round to visit | Tell his friend the toy was broken | Only play with toys alone
exercise: Isla took all the best pencils from the class box. Who noticed? | The others, who were left with the broken ones | Nobody at all, because nobody minds about pencils | Only the caretaker | The pencil company
exercise: Tom was jealous when his friend won the art prize and said she cheated. Why was this wrong? | It was untrue and hurt her because he was jealous | She should have let him win, since he wanted it more | Art prizes are always unfair | Saying it is fine if you whisper
exercise: What would put Tom's accusation right? | Saying sorry to her and saying well done | Saying he was only joking | Saying all prizes are silly | Entering next year and saying she cheated again
exercise: Grace pushed to be first in the queue and a boy fell over. What was the fair consequence? | She went to the back and said sorry | She got to go first anyway, as she was quick | The boy she pushed went to the back instead | Nothing, because queues have no real rules
exercise: Ben hid the best sweets in his drawer so his sister would not get them. She found them. What happened next? | He had to share them with her | He was allowed to keep all of them | Mum went and bought more sweets for him | His sister hid them back in her room
exercise: Finn's friend says «you ate the sweets at the last party too and nobody said anything». What is true? | Others still went without, even if nobody said so | It means sweets are for whoever is fastest at the table | It proves it was fair | It means nobody minded
test: «there's 12 sweets and 6 of us, i had 8 lol». What's the problem? | The others only have 4 left to share between 5 | Everyone still gets 2 each, since the bag was big enough | There is no problem, 8 is fine | He should have taken all 12
test: bro grabs the biggest pizza slice every time «cos i'm the oldest innit». Fair? | No, being oldest does not mean the biggest share | Yes, the oldest always gets the most, that's fair | Yes, if he grabs it fast | Only on weekends
test: mate says «her trainers are rank» but he's been staring at them all week. what's going on? | He's probably envious and saying it to feel better | He wants her to wear them more often, so he says it | He really hates trainers | He's being kind to her
test: «she only won cos she cheated, everyone knows». You saw her do it all herself. What do you say? | No, she did it fairly, well done to her | Yeah, she probably cheated | Say nothing and nod | Tell the teacher she cheated too
test: Teacher: «Who's got all the felt tips? Half the class has none!» Isla has them in her tray. Best move? | Put them back and say sorry | Hide them in her bag until home time | Say the felt tips were hers all along | Blame the boy sitting next to her
test: shoving to get to the front, kid falls and cuts his knee. «not my fault he's slow». What's true? | Pushing caused the fall; she should say sorry and wait her turn | It's his fault for being so slow at the front, he should have moved | Queues are first come, first shove | Cuts don't count as hurt
test: my sister hid the best chocolates last christmas and «it worked last time, so it's fine». Is it? | No, it's still unfair, and next time she may be found out | Yes, hiding is allowed at Christmas, everyone does it with sweets | Yes, if it worked once it's fine | Only if she eats them quickly
test: little bro cried cos u wouldn't share ur new drone. Mum: «Share or it goes back in the box». What's the wise choice? | Let him have a turn and show him how | Keep it and let it go in the box | Fly it out of his reach | Say it's broken
test: Party mum says «just one goody bag each, kids». Finn takes two. What's likely to happen? | The last child gets none and Finn has to give one back | Nobody will count, so it all works out fine | Everyone gets two | The mum will make more bags
test: «why should i let her choose first, i'm the one who wants the big bit». What makes letting her choose first fair? | It puts right the times you grabbed first | It makes you look silly | It means you never get cake | It's only for babies
exam: Identify the principal consequence of one participant appropriating a disproportionate quantity of a shared provision. | Other participants are left with an insufficient share or none | All participants nonetheless receive an equal quantity of the provision | The provision is replenished automatically | The quantity of the provision increases
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises an unfounded accusation of cheating made from envy? | It is untrue and causes harm to the person accused | It is a reasonable reaction to defeat | It is justified if the speaker lost | It is harmless if made privately
exam: It is given that a child has, on a prior occasion, concealed preferred confectionery without detection. Select the conclusion consistent with sound reasoning. | The absence of detection does not render the concealment fair | The prior success establishes a right to conceal on future occasions | Concealment is permitted during festivals | Detection is the sole measure of fairness
exam: State the remedy most consistent with making amends after appropriating the largest portion of a shared cake. | An apology and permitting the other party to choose first thereafter | A demand for a larger cake | A refusal to discuss the matter | The consumption of the remaining portions
